# The SRS specifies fuller ~200–300-word attraction descriptions. These additional
# original visitor-oriented paragraphs explain context without inventing entry policies.
EXTRA_CONTEXT = {
 'Gangaramaya Temple': 'For a one-day itinerary, Gangaramaya works particularly well as a cultural stop in the centre of the city. A visitor can take in the temple atmosphere first, then continue to Beira Lake or a nearby modern landmark without crossing the entire metropolitan area. The mixture of everyday devotion, careful craftsmanship, and historical collections means different visitors may notice different things: some focus on architecture, others on the stories connected with the displayed objects. Plan a slower visit if this is your main heritage interest, and a shorter visit if you have several stops. As at any active shrine, an event or observance may change normal access. The practical aim of this listing is to help you arrive prepared and make a respectful decision about whether it fits your day.',
 'Independence Memorial Hall': 'The memorial is an especially useful stop for understanding the transition between the colonial period and independent Sri Lanka. It does not require the same time commitment as a large museum, making it a sensible choice when planning a morning or afternoon with several nearby attractions. Visitors who enjoy photographing architecture can look at the proportions of the roof and columns, while those interested in history can pay attention to the monument’s commemorative role. Its lawns and open surroundings offer some breathing room during an otherwise urban visit. Do not confuse access to the outdoor hall and square with opening hours for the museum below the monument. Because special state occasions can affect access, confirm any arrangements if you intend to visit on a national holiday.',
 'Viharamahadevi Park': 'A park stop is a practical part of a balanced day itinerary: it gives you time to walk without queues, recharge between indoor attractions, and enjoy the greener side of Colombo. There is no need to follow a fixed route through the grounds. Instead, choose a short loop appropriate to your energy level, the weather, and the time you have left. The setting can be particularly useful for families or visitors who prefer nature and recreation over a full day of monuments. Its location near the museum district also makes it easy to combine with a cultural visit. Remember that a public park can host maintenance work and temporary activities, so particular facilities may not always be available. Take breaks in shaded areas during hot weather.',
 'National Museum of Colombo': 'Because the museum contains a variety of subjects, visitors can adapt the experience to their interests and schedule. Someone interested mainly in early history may spend more time on archaeological displays, while another visitor might focus on decorative arts or royal heritage. Rather than attempting to see everything in a short stop, choose the rooms that matter to you and allow time to read their explanations. An indoor museum can also provide relief from midday heat, so scheduling it around the middle of the day often makes sense. Public holidays and gallery closures may affect access, and admission charges differ by visitor category. Check the official museum pages shortly before travelling and keep some extra time for tickets, moving between galleries, and a brief rest afterward.',
 'Dutch Hospital Shopping Precinct': 'The attraction is best understood as a historic setting with a contemporary purpose, rather than a preserved hospital operating as a museum. The contrast between old buildings and modern shops offers a simple introduction to the layered history of Colombo Fort. A short stop is enough to walk through the courtyard, but travellers who plan to eat or meet companions might want much longer. If your day already includes a Fort architectural walk, pairing this location with the nearby Old Parliament Building saves unnecessary cross-city travel. Many visitors prefer the precinct later in the day for its restaurants and atmosphere, although individual businesses do not necessarily follow one standard timetable. Consider it a flexible stop rather than relying on a guaranteed meal service or a specific store being open.',
 'Galle Face Green': 'A walk here can be as simple as watching the waves and the people using the promenade, or as lively as trying food from stalls. The experience changes with the time of day, weather, and crowd size. An early visit is usually quieter, while an evening visit often feels more social. For a compact tourist itinerary, the location pairs well with the Fort district because the distance between the two areas is relatively small. Keep a flexible allowance for the return journey if roads are busy at sunset. The Green is an exposed seaside space, so carry water and consider the weather rather than treating the promenade as an indoor or sheltered attraction. Swimming conditions at this urban waterfront must never be assumed safe.',
 'Jami Ul-Alfar Mosque': 'The Red Mosque also helps visitors understand the diversity of religious traditions present in central Colombo. Its setting within Pettah’s trading streets makes it easy to include with a short market walk, but navigating the busy area may take longer than expected. There is an important difference between seeing the exterior and being admitted for a visitor tour; access to worship areas is managed by the mosque. Visitors with limited time can appreciate the street-facing architecture without requesting entry, while those hoping to learn more should use the mosque’s official visitor guidance. Allow extra time for traffic, pedestrian crowds, and respectful interaction with staff. Keep personal belongings secure as you would in any busy commercial district, and never treat an active service as a sightseeing performance.',
 'Diyasaru Park': 'For a visitor whose interests lean toward nature and wellness, Diyasaru can be the centrepiece of a half-day around the Kotte and Thalawathugoda area. It offers a very different rhythm from the bustling city centre. Rather than trying to cover every path quickly, pause at observation areas and look for movement in the vegetation and water. The visit duration in this app is only a planning suggestion; birdwatchers may want longer, while other travellers may enjoy a shorter circuit. As wetlands are sensitive ecosystems, remain on permitted paths and avoid disturbing wildlife. Combine this park with nearby Beddagana only if you have allowed enough time for both, including local transfers. Check the park website for ticketing, visitor notices, and any weather-related changes.',
 'Lotus Tower': 'The tower is particularly useful for visitors who want a visual understanding of Colombo’s geography after visiting different neighbourhoods. From an elevated vantage point, the relationship between lakes, roads, skyline, and coastline becomes easier to appreciate. It can also provide a memorable end to a city sightseeing day if the observation areas are open. However, the published operating timetable is not the same as guaranteed entry at any moment; ticket desks can close earlier, capacities can change, and maintenance or special events can affect visitor access. Budget time for queues and transitions through the site. A visit here may not suit someone avoiding heights. Use the official website for current ticket categories and opening information rather than relying on old social-media prices.',
 'Sri Kailasanathar Swami Temple': 'This stop is valuable for travellers who want to see living Hindu traditions alongside the Buddhist, Islamic, and colonial heritage found elsewhere in Colombo. The colours and sculptural detailing may attract architecture enthusiasts, but the temple’s most important role is religious. Allow yourself enough time to approach respectfully and to follow any instructions about shoes, dress, or areas where photography is restricted. The midday break is a major planning consideration: a timetable that works for a museum may not work for this temple. If you expect to arrive near closing, choose another stop first or confirm access directly. The surrounding street layout can also be confusing for first-time visitors, so check the Captain’s Garden address carefully rather than assuming every similarly named shrine is the same location.',
 'Old Parliament Building': 'For a visitor interested in government and the history of modern Sri Lanka, this building provides an important visual link between the island’s colonial institutions and later national political life. The experience is primarily an outdoor architectural viewing stop, so a short time allocation is usually enough. Because it is an active government area, public access rules and security arrangements take priority over tourism. Do not enter restricted property or attempt to photograph security-sensitive areas. Visitors can instead appreciate the proportions and façade from lawful public viewpoints before continuing through Fort. The nearby Dutch Hospital precinct can offer a place to rest or eat after the walk. Like other urban landmarks, its surroundings may be disrupted by road controls or civic events, so allow some flexibility in your travel plan.',
 'Seema Malaka Temple': 'This is a good choice for visitors who want a quiet pause amid busy streets or who are interested in the work of Geoffrey Bawa. Its low horizontal structures, reflections on the water, and surrounding high-rise buildings create an unusually calm setting in the centre of Colombo. A short visit can be memorable, especially when paired with the larger Gangaramaya temple a short distance away. If you want time for photography or quiet observation, consider extending the suggested stay. The site is not a recreation pier and the water platforms should be used respectfully. Religious events may change the normal visiting experience. Bring suitable clothing, protect yourself from midday sun reflected off the lake, and check any combined ticket arrangements if you plan to visit both linked temple locations.',
 'Beddagana Wetland Park': 'Beddagana is well suited to visitors interested in ecological landscapes, birds, or low-key outdoor exercise. The attraction is not simply an urban garden: water levels, habitat conditions, and the time of day can noticeably change what wildlife is visible. A patient walk along its designated paths is usually more rewarding than rushing through the area. For family groups, the nature setting can provide an educational contrast with museums and major buildings. It is also convenient for itineraries that spend more time east of central Colombo, closer to the Kaduwela starting point. If rainfall is expected, check whether walking routes are open and suitable. Keep noise low in birdwatching areas, take litter away with you, and follow the instructions of park staff.',
 'Colombo City Centre': 'In a full sightseeing day, an indoor shopping centre can be a practical logistical stop. It offers a place to rest, eat, find basic services, or wait out a rain shower, although the availability of each service depends on the businesses operating there. Travellers with children may appreciate the flexibility, and visitors interested in the changing urban character of Colombo may enjoy seeing modern retail development near the historic lake district. The mall also works as an easy pairing with Seema Malaka; the two attractions sit relatively close to one another. This app gives a one-hour visit as a convenient default, not a recommendation that you must shop for exactly that long. If you plan a cinema visit or a particular restaurant meal, confirm that venue’s hours separately.',
 'Saskia Fernando Gallery': 'A gallery visit can bring a thoughtful creative element into an otherwise history- or architecture-focused itinerary. Contemporary art often offers fresh ways to think about society, identity, place, and everyday life, although each exhibition will have its own theme. The most useful preparation is to check what show is currently installed and whether the venue has special opening arrangements. Visitors need not have formal art training to enjoy looking closely at works and reading exhibition notes. The gallery is likely to be a relatively short stop, so it can fit between the nearby museum district and central Colombo attractions. Because exhibitions and locations may change, do not assume an older address or opening schedule will always apply. Ask permission before photographing artworks and respect any instructions from gallery staff.'
}

for _item in PLACES:
    _item['description'] = _item['description'] + '\n\n' + EXTRA_CONTEXT[_item['name']]
